agent	Parse MCP Gateway logs for step summary	2026-10-21T17:46:29.3116057Z {"timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:46:18.993Z","event":"rpc_response","_schema":"rpc-message/v2","direction":"IN","server_id":"safeoutputs","payload":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"result\":\"success\"}"}]}}}
agent	Parse MCP Gateway logs for step summary	2026-10-21T17:46:29.3121724Z {"timestamp":"2026-10-21T17:46:21.153Z","event":"rpc_request","_schema":"rpc-message/v2","direction":"OUT","server_id":"safeoutputs","method":"tools/call","payload":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"tools/call","params":{"arguments":{"body":"Thanks for the report! This is an automated first-pass triage; a maintainer will make the final call.\n\n**Reproduction:**\n- Black 25.1.1: not run, because that release did not exist on the issue date, so the script refuses it.\n- Black 25.11.0 (latest on the issue date), default flags, snippet from the report: crash (`INTERNAL ERROR: ... produced code that is not equivalent to the source`).\n\n**Assessment:** The crash reproduces with the latest release. The input has two trailing comments (`# pyright: ignore[...]` and `# type: ignore[operator]`) inside one parenthesized expression. The AST diff in the report shows a `TypeIgnore` node disappearing, which suggests the `# type: ignore` comment is mishandled during line splitting. I haven't confirmed the root cause.\n\n**Next step:** A maintainer should confirm this and look at how `# type: ignore` comments are handled in `linegen.py`. Reducing the snippet further, for example by removing the `pyright` comment, would help narrow it down. No duplicate candidate looked like the same problem.\n\n\u003csub\u003eblack-issue-triage skill v1.0.0 · suggested for maintainers: none\u003c/sub\u003e"},"name":"add_comment"}}}
